// The syntax tree, and GAP 1's real evidence.

export enum Operator {
	ADD,
	SUBTRACT,
	MULTIPLY,
	EQUALS,
}

Operator implements Copy

// FINDING 1 — this is the recursive value type `examples/QuadTree` was supposed
// to be evidence for, and was not.
//
// QuadTree's GAP 1 says a recursive value type has no size, and
// `tree-places.elp` FINDING 1 found that QuadTree's own enum does not actually
// demonstrate it: both variants hold an array, an array must be a heap buffer
// to grow, so the indirection is already there and the type lays out fine.
//
// `Expr` has no such escape. `BINARY` holds two `Expr` **directly**, so the
// size equation is `size(Expr) = tag + 2 * size(Expr)`, which has no finite
// solution. `LET` and `IF` are the same shape. There is no array anywhere to
// supply the indirection, and an AST is the single most common recursive type
// any language compiles.
//
// `memory-model.md` has `Copy`, `Move`, `Protected` and `Binding<T>`, and not
// one of them says "boxed". Rust writes `Box<Expr>`, Swift sidesteps it by
// making the recursive case a `class`, C++ uses a pointer, and OCaml and
// Haskell box everything so the question never arises.
//
// Written without a marker — the version a person reaches for first, and the
// one the compiler has to reject with a diagnostic that teaches the fix.
export enum Expr {
	LITERAL { of int },
	NAME { of string },

	BINARY { op Operator, left Expr, right Expr },

	// `LET` binds and does not permit the bound name inside its own value.
	// `LETREC` does. Keeping them separate is what makes FINDING 4 provable
	// rather than a matter of how the evaluator happens to be written.
	LET { name string, value Expr, body Expr },
	LETREC { name string, value Expr, body Expr },

	LAMBDA { parameter string, body Expr },
	CALL { callee Expr, argument Expr },

	IF { condition Expr, then Expr, otherwise Expr },
}

// vim: ft=kotlin
